  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Common Goldspeck is a bright yellow crustose lichen belonging to the Candelariaceae family, easily recognized by its vibrant coloration and granular texture. It is widespread throughout New England and other temperate regions, thriving on various substrates including bark, wood, and stone.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Bright yellow color",
      "description": "Distinctive golden-yellow to bright yellow thallus that is highly visible on tree bark and rocks",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Granular crust",
      "description": "Fine, powdery to granular surface texture with a somewhat uneven, bumpy appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small fruiting bodies",
      "description": "Small, orange-yellow apothecia (fruiting structures) may be present on the thallus surface",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Common Goldspeck grows on bark of deciduous and coniferous trees, wood fences, roof shingles, and occasionally on stone surfaces throughout New England, preferring areas with moderate light and moisture.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The bright yellow pigment in Common Goldspeck comes from compounds called anthraquinones, which serve as natural sunscreen and give the lichen its striking appearance that makes it one of the most conspicuous lichens in eastern forests.",
